comment14.text=\r\n Turn all the wheels on the machine.\r\n
comment15.target=java.lang.String[] symbols()
comment15.text=\r\n List the colors of all the machines\r\n
comment16.target=int distinctSymbols()
comment16.text=\r\n Number of distinct colors currently showing across all wheels (not\r\n how many colors exist in their inventories). A freshly added wheel\r\n that has not spun or been placed yet shows no color, so it is not\r\n counted.\r\n
comment17.target=java.lang.String[] configuration()
comment17.text=\r\n Visible colors on each wheel, from left to right.\r\n
comment18.target=boolean isJackpot()
comment18.text=\r\n true if all wheels display the same symbol.\r\n This is a plain, deterministic comparison of the current\r\n configuration\: it does not depend on chance in any way, only the\r\n spin that produced that configuration does (see Wheel.spin()).\r\n
comment19.target=boolean ok()
comment19.text=\r\n @return true if the last operation was succeed\r\n
comment2.params=pacer
comment2.target=SlotMachine(StepPacer)
comment2.text=\r\n Constructor for objects of class SlotMachine, letting the caller\r\n decide how the step-by-step animation in spin(wheel, steps) is\r\n paced.\r\n\r\n SlotMachine's own logic never depends on Thread.sleep, Canvas, or\r\n any other concrete pacing mechanism\: it only depends on the\r\n StepPacer interface. This constructor exists so a different pacer\r\n (a different animation mechanism, or a test double that does not\r\n really wait) can be plugged in later without touching spin().\r\n\r\n @param pacer used to pause between animation steps.\r\n
comment20.target=void succeed()
comment21.params=reason
comment21.target=void fail(java.lang.String)
comment21.text=\r\n Marks the last operation as failed and, if the simulator is\r\n visible, shows the reason to the user through a JOptionPane.\r\n
comment22.target=void announceJackpotIfReached()
comment22.text=\r\n If the machine is visible and just reached a winning configuration,\r\n congratulate the user through a JOptionPane.\r\n
comment23.target=void rebuildShapes()
comment23.text=\r\n (Re)builds the machine's body, the jackpot light and one square per\r\n wheel. Called whenever the number of wheels changes.\r\n
comment24.target=void refreshShapes()
comment24.text=\r\n Repaints the wheels' squares, the jackpot light and the machine's\r\n body to match the current configuration.\r\n
comment25.target=void syncVisualState()
comment25.text=\r\n Shows or hides every shape according to the visible flag, and keeps\r\n the jackpot light on only while the machine is visible and won.\r\n
comment26.params=value min max
comment26.target=int clamp(int, int, int)
comment27.target=void exit()
comment28.params=wheel1 wheel2
comment28.target=void swap(int, int)
comment28.text=\r\n exchanges the positions of two roulette wheels. \r\n It temporarily saves the first wheel in memory, moves the second wheel \r\n into the first wheel's slot, and \r\n then assigns the saved copy of the first wheel to the second wheel's original \r\n slot.\r\n
comment29.params=wheel
comment29.target=void lock(int)
comment29.text=\r\n Locks a wheel\r\n
comment3.params=n
comment3.target=SlotMachine(int)
comment3.text=\r\n Constructor for objects of class SlotMachine with n wheels and n\r\n distinct symbols (one shared palette of n colors, placed on every\r\n wheel), each wheel randomly spun to an initial position. This is\r\n the constructor used to set up an instance of the marat\u00F3n problem\:\r\n "create a machine with an equal number of wheels and symbols".\r\n\r\n @param n number of wheels (and, matching each other, the size of\r\n          the shared color palette every wheel gets). Values above\r\n          the size of the internal color pool (50) are clamped down\r\n          to it, since there are no more distinct CSS colors to draw\r\n          more symbols with.\r\n
comment30.params=wheel
comment30.target=void unlock(int)
comment30.text=\r\n Unlocks a wheel\r\n
comment31.params=wheel steps
comment31.target=void spin(int, int)
comment31.text=\r\n Rotates the indicated wheel forward the given number of steps. If\r\n the machine is visible, the motion is shown one step at a time\r\n (with a short pause between steps) instead of jumping straight to\r\n the final symbol.\r\n
comment32.params=setSymbols
comment32.target=void spin(java.lang.String[])
comment32.text=\r\n Sets the exact configuration of the machine\: applies one color per\r\n wheel, skipping any wheel that is locked. Mainly useful for testing\r\n isJackpot() deterministically, without depending on chance.\r\n
comment4.params=n
comment4.target=java.lang.String[] randomDistinctColors(int)
comment4.text=\r\n Picks n distinct color names out of COLOR_PALETTE, in random order.\r\n @param n how many distinct colors to pick; clamped to the palette size.\r\n
comment5.params=pos
comment5.target=void addWheel(int)
comment5.text=\r\n Adds an empty wheel on the indicated position.\r\n
